A canvas's `@click` reaches its handler on fyne and gtk4.

Neither wired one. fyne draws a canvas as a `canvas.Image` or `canvas.Raster`,
which Fyne never hands a tap: it goes only to a widget implementing
`fyne.Tappable`, so a clickable canvas is shown in a `fynelayout.Tap` and its
container holds that in the canvas's place. gtk4 draws one in a
`GtkDrawingArea`, which has no click signal; a `GtkGestureClick` added as its
controller does, and its `pressed` passes `(n_press, x, y)` ahead of the
user_data the connect trampoline reads, so it has a trampoline of its own.

No Fyne or GTK signature replaces the handler's SNGL parameter, so it keeps it
and is handed the declared payload, a `ClickEvent`. That is why a payload named
by a handler is declared: before, the handler said `ClickEvent` and nothing
emitted the type. `ClickEvent` has no fields -- nothing reports where the click
landed -- so a test may write `click()` or `click({})` and the Go invoker takes
nothing.

A test's `c.board` is found for a canvas written `draw.canvas #board`: the
lookup resolved only a bare component name, so a node written through an import
alias was `no member "board" on component main`.

The canvases under the loop are instances, whose click is an event their record
relays; they ride along to hold that path to the same wiring.

bubbletea and android wire no canvas click either and are left out.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `Add\(m\.board\)` -- the container holds the Tap, not the image a tap never reaches
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:test"
import draw "sngl:ui/draw"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var hits = 0
    var xs = [1, 2]

    vbox {
        text(value="hits {hits}")
        draw.canvas #board(width=40px, height=40px, @click(e) { hits += 1 }) {
            draw.circle(cx=20.0, cy=20.0, r=10.0)
        }
        for var x = xs {
            draw.canvas(width=40px, height=40px, @click(e) { hits += x }) {
                draw.circle(cx=20.0, cy=20.0, r=float(x) * 5.0)
            }
        }
    }
}

func testClickCounts(t Test, c main) {
    c.board.click()
    t.assert(c.hits == 1)
    c.board.click({})
    t.assert(c.hits == 2)
}

window {
    main
}
